@@s001 Classes and dynamic dispatch
@@s002 due:static type (compiler),dato:real object (execution),ora:where the method is looked for,ok:printed
@@s003 Two classes: B extends A and redefines f (with <code>override</code>).
@@s004 <code>val b1 = B()</code> creates an object of class B. It will remain a B forever.
@@s005 <code>val a2: A = b1</code>: the variable has type <b>A</b> (upcast), but points to the same B object.
@@s006 <code>a2.f()</code>: the <b>compiler</b> looks at the static type A and only checks that f exists in A. It exists: it compiles.
@@s007 At <b>execution</b> the JVM starts from the class of the real object, B, and there finds f: it prints <b>B</b>. It is dynamic dispatch. (Also the val g redefined in B prints B, checked with Scala 3.8.)
@@s008 dato:field,ora:run at creation,ok:result
@@s009 dato:declared type (compiler),ora:real object (runtime),ok:printed
@@s010 Classes and dynamic dispatch
@@s011 Two object-oriented programming topics with which week 3 opens: the <b>primary constructor</b> (a Java class of 15 lines becomes one line) and <b>dynamic dispatch</b> (which method is really called when a subclass redefines it).
@@s012 The primary constructor
@@s013 In Scala the constructor parameters are written <b>right after the class name</b>. The whole rest of the class body is run when the object is created.
@@s014 Scala
@@s015 The same in Java
@@s016 What each piece does
@@s017 The constructor parameters are on the first line.
@@s018 <code>val</code> in front of the parameter turns it into a <b>public read-only field</b>: from outside you can write <code>p.name</code>. Without <code>val</code> the parameter is used only inside the class.
@@s019 The statements in the class body are run <b>at every creation</b>: they are the body of the constructor.
@@s020 A method that uses the fields. <code>$name</code> in the string is the short form of <code>${name}</code>.
@@s021 Creation: in Scala 3 <code>new</code> is not needed. It prints <i>Just constructed a person</i> (checked).
@@s022 Primary constructors are used with <code>case class</code> and with the <code>case</code>s of <code>enum</code>: <code>Cons(head: A, tail: List[A])</code> is exactly a primary constructor.
@@s023 Person(
@@s024 Poll: dynamic dispatch in Java
@@s025 ((Printable) y).hello(), step by step
@@s026 The object created is a <b>Triangle</b>. It will remain a Triangle forever.
@@s027 The cast tells the compiler "treat it as a Printable". It serves to check the types <b>before</b> execution.
@@s028 
@@s029 At <b>runtime</b> the JVM looks at the real object and searches for <code>hello</code> starting from its class.
@@s030 This is <b>dynamic dispatch</b> (or virtual call).
@@s031 In Scala
@@s032 As in Java, Python and JavaScript, <b>all instance methods are virtual</b>: they are chosen on the basis of the real object. (In C# and C++ instead no, unless you declare it.)
@@s033 <b>What does it print?</b> Answer: <b>square square triangle triangle</b>. The method is chosen on the basis of the <b>real</b> class of the object, decided with <code>new</code>, not on the basis of the type of the variable or of the cast. The cast <code>(Printable) x</code> only changes what the compiler lets you call, not the object.
@@s034 Four calls, the real object decides
@@s035 Methods and function-values: <code>DynamicDispatch.scala</code>
@@s036 The week 3 file compares a method <code>def f()</code> with a field <code>val g</code> that contains a function.
@@s037 Static type and real object
@@s038 03-option/DynamicDispatch.scala (shortened)
@@s039 In <code>A</code>: a method <code>f</code> and a <b>field</b> <code>g</code> whose value is a function.
@@s040 <code>B</code> redefines both. <code>override</code> is mandatory when you redefine something that already exists.
@@s041 <code>a2</code> has type <code>A</code> but the object is a <code>B</code> (upcast).
@@s042 Prints <b>B</b>: dynamic dispatch on the method.
@@s043 It also prints <b>B</b> (checked with Scala 3.8). Reading a <code>val</code> goes through a hidden method that returns the field, and that too is virtual. The comment in the course file speaks of "hiding": in practice the result is the same as <code>f</code>.
